Specification Accounts

Description The chart of accounts, and what an account balance means.
Details SimpleAccount keeps one tree of accounts. The types that hold money and
Details the types Quicken calls categories are both nodes in that tree, which is
Details what allows every transaction to balance. See Transactions.
Details
Details An account has no Name attribute. The name is the last segment of the
Details path, and is derived by the rule below rather than stored twice.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The account
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Entity Account
Uses AccountPath and AccountType from CoreTypes
| Attribute   | Type        | Default | Notes                               |
| Path        | AccountPath |         | Unique; identifies the account      |
| Type        | AccountType | Checking | Fixed once the account has postings |
| Placeholder | Boolean     | false   | A grouping node; takes no postings  |
| Hidden      | Boolean     | false   | Kept out of pickers, history kept   |
| Alias       | Text        | none    | A short way to type it; see finding a category |
| PaymentPayee | Text       | none    | What a payment to this account is called on the paying account's statement; see ImportOfx |
| Bank        | Text        | none    | Which institution it is at; see what is kept about an account |
| AccountNumber | Text      | none    | The number on the statement, kept as written |

Collection ChartOfAccounts
Description Every account in the book, in one tree.
| DataType | Minimum | Maximum | Notes          |
| Account  | 0       | 100000  | Path is unique |

BusinessRule The name of an account is the last segment of its path
Description A root account has no parent, so its parent is empty.
Examples: AccountNameDerivation
| Path                    | Name             | Parent        |
| Expenses                | Expenses         |               |
| Expenses:Auto           | Auto             | Expenses      |
| Expenses:Auto:Fuel      | Fuel             | Expenses:Auto |
| Equity:Opening Balances | Opening Balances | Equity        |

Attributes AccountNameDerivation
| Attribute | Type        | Default |
| Path      | AccountPath |         |
| Name      | AccountName |         |
| Parent    | Text        | none    |

Background A small chart of accounts
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                    | Type       | Placeholder |
| Assets                  | Checking   | true        |
| Assets:Checking         | Checking   | false       |
| Assets:Savings          | Checking   | false       |
| Liabilities             | OtherDebt  | true        |
| Liabilities:Visa        | CreditCard | false       |
| Income                  | Income     | true        |
| Income:Salary           | Income     | false       |
| Expenses                | Expense    | true        |
| Expenses:Groceries      | Expense    | false       |
| Expenses:Auto           | Expense    | true        |
| Expenses:Auto:Fuel      | Expense    | false       |
| Expenses:Auto:Repairs   | Expense    | false       |
| Equity:Opening Balances | Equity     | false       |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Creating accounts
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

$WIP
Scenario Adding an account under a parent
Given the chart of accounts is as previous
When account added : Account Vertical
| Path | Expenses:Auto:Insurance |
| Type | Expense                 |
Then account is : Account Vertical
| Path        | Expenses:Auto:Insurance |
| Type        | Expense                 |
| Placeholder | false                   |
| Hidden      | false                   |

$WIP
Scenario A missing parent is created as a placeholder
Description Importing twenty years of data names deep categories that were
Description never created by hand, so the missing levels are filled in and
Description marked as placeholders.
Given the chart of accounts is as previous
When account added : Account Vertical
| Path | Expenses:Home:Garden:Seeds |
| Type | Expense                    |
Then accounts created are : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                       | Type    | Placeholder |
| Expenses:Home              | Expense | true        |
| Expenses:Home:Garden       | Expense | true        |
| Expenses:Home:Garden:Seeds | Expense | false       |
# The first two were filled in; the third is the one that was asked for.

$WIP
Scenario A filled-in parent takes the type of its child
Description A placeholder created on the way to an Expense is itself an Expense,
Description so the roll-up in a report adds up.
Given the chart of accounts is as previous
When account added : Account Vertical
| Path | Income:Interest:Bank Interest |
| Type | Income                        |
Then accounts created are : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                          | Type   | Placeholder |
| Income:Interest               | Income | true        |
| Income:Interest:Bank Interest | Income | false       |

Scenario Two accounts may not share a path
Given the chart of accounts is as previous
When account added : Account Vertical
| Path | Assets:Checking |
| Type | Checking        |
Then rejected because : Rejection
| Reason                                  |
| An account named Assets:Checking exists |

Attributes Rejection
| Attribute | Type | Default |
| Reason    | Text |         |

Scenario The same name under two different parents is fine
Description Expenses:Auto:Insurance and Expenses:Home:Insurance are two
Description categories, and both are named Insurance.
Given the chart of accounts is as previous
When account added : Account Vertical
| Path | Expenses:Auto:Insurance |
| Type | Expense                 |
When account added : Account Vertical
| Path | Expenses:Home:Insurance |
| Type | Expense                 |
Then accounts named Insurance are : ChartOfAccounts CompareOnly
| Path                    |
| Expenses:Auto:Insurance |
| Expenses:Home:Insurance |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Renaming and moving
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Scenario Renaming an account renames its descendants
Description The path of a child is built from the path of its parent, so a
Description rename rewrites every path beneath it. Postings are untouched:
Description they refer to the account, not to the text of its path.
Given the chart of accounts is as previous
When account renamed : AccountRename Vertical
| Path    | Expenses:Auto |
| NewName | Vehicle       |
Then accounts are : ChartOfAccounts CompareOnly
| Path                     |
| Expenses:Vehicle         |
| Expenses:Vehicle:Fuel    |
| Expenses:Vehicle:Repairs |

Attributes AccountRename
| Attribute | Type        | Default |
| Path      | AccountPath |         |
| NewName   | AccountName |         |

Scenario Moving an account to another parent
Given the chart of accounts is as previous
When account moved : AccountMove Vertical
| Path      | Expenses:Auto:Fuel |
| NewParent | Expenses           |
Then accounts are : ChartOfAccounts CompareOnly
| Path          |
| Expenses:Fuel |

Attributes AccountMove
| Attribute | Type        | Default |
| Path      | AccountPath |         |
| NewParent | AccountPath |         |

Scenario An account may not be moved beneath itself
Given the chart of accounts is as previous
When account moved : AccountMove Vertical
| Path      | Expenses:Auto      |
| NewParent | Expenses:Auto:Fuel |
Then rejected because : Rejection
| Reason                                   |
| An account may not be its own descendant |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Deleting and hiding
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Scenario An account with postings is not deleted
Description Twenty years of history is the point of the program, so nothing
Description silently discards a posting. Hide the account instead.
Given the chart of accounts is as previous
Given Expenses:Groceries has postings : PostingCount
| Count |
| 412   |
When account deleted : AccountSelect
| Path               |
| Expenses:Groceries |
Then rejected because : Rejection
| Reason                                               |
| Expenses:Groceries has 412 postings; hide it instead |

Attributes PostingCount
| Attribute | Type    | Default |
| Count     | Integer | 0       |

Scenario An account with no postings is deleted
Given the chart of accounts is as previous
Given Expenses:Auto:Repairs has postings : PostingCount
| Count |
| 0     |
When account deleted : AccountSelect
| Path                  |
| Expenses:Auto:Repairs |
Then accounts under Expenses:Auto are : ChartOfAccounts CompareOnly
| Path               |
| Expenses:Auto:Fuel |

$WIP
Scenario Hiding an account keeps its history
Given the chart of accounts is as previous
When account hidden : AccountSelect
| Path               |
| Expenses:Groceries |
Then account is : Account Vertical
| Path        | Expenses:Groceries |
| Type        | Expense            |
| Placeholder | false              |
| Hidden      | true               |
Then category picker offers : ChartOfAccounts CompareOnly
| Path                  |
| Expenses:Auto:Fuel    |
| Expenses:Auto:Repairs |
# Expenses:Groceries is absent from the picker, and its 412 postings still
# report under it.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What identifies an account
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

$WIP
BusinessRule An account is identified by something that is not its path
Description The path is a label. It is what people read, what they type and what
Description every table in this specification names an account by, and it can
Description change: a category can be renamed, and it can be moved under a
Description different parent. What identifies the account does not change when
Description either of those happens.
Details The identifier is the one the transactions use, from the same generator:
Details the moment the account was created, to the millisecond. One scheme for
Details both is easier to reason about than two, and accounts are made rarely
Details enough that waiting a millisecond for one costs nothing.
Details
Details It is deliberately absent from every table in this specification. Nobody
Details types it, nobody reads it, and a scenario that stated one would be
Details stating something no scenario controls. Accounts are named here by path,
Details which is what a reader understands.
Details
Details What this buys is that rearranging categories is a small operation. With
Details the path as the identity, moving Auto:Fuel under Vehicle means rewriting
Details every posting that named it -- tens of thousands of rows for one
Details decision, and a book that refers to a category that no longer exists if
Details it stops half way. With the path derived from the tree, the same move
Details changes which parent the account points at, and the paths are worked out
Details again afterwards. No posting is touched, because no posting ever named
Details the path in the first place.
Examples: AccountRearranged
| WhatChanges              | ThePath | TheIdentity | ThePostings |
| Renaming a category      | Yes     | No          | Untouched   |
| Moving it under another  | Yes     | No          | Untouched   |
| Hiding it                | No      | No          | Untouched   |
| Changing its type        | No      | No          | Untouched   |

Attributes AccountRearranged
| Attribute    | Type   | Default |
| WhatChanges  | String |         |
| ThePath      | Text   | No      |
| TheIdentity  | Text   | No      |
| ThePostings  | Text   | Untouched |

$WIP
Scenario Moving a category under another parent leaves its postings alone
Description The postings named the account, not the path, so moving it is a
Description change to one account and to nothing else. The paths underneath are
Description worked out again, which is why Fuel reads as Vehicle:Fuel
Description afterwards without anything having rewritten it.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking        | Checking | false       |
| Expenses:Auto          | Expense | true        |
| Expenses:Auto:Fuel     | Expense | false       |
| Expenses:Vehicle       | Expense | true        |
Given postings are : PostingLine
| Account            | Amount |
| Assets:Checking    | -40.00 |
| Expenses:Auto:Fuel | 40.00  |
When account moved under : AccountMove Vertical
| Path      | Expenses:Auto:Fuel |
| NewParent | Expenses:Vehicle   |
Then the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts CompareOnly
| Path                  |
| Expenses:Vehicle:Fuel |
Then balances are : AccountBalance CompareOnly
| Account               | RawBalance |
| Expenses:Vehicle:Fuel | 40.00      |
# The forty pounds did not move and was never rewritten: the posting names the
# account, and the account is the same account.

Attributes AccountMove
| Attribute | Type        | Default |
| Path      | AccountPath |         |
| NewParent | AccountPath |         |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What is kept about an account
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Description Each account has a dialog of its own, holding what is known about
Description the account itself: which bank it is at, and the number on the
Description statement.
Details These are facts about the account and not about any transaction, so
Details they live on the account rather than in a setting shared by the book.
Details Two current accounts at the same bank have to be told apart, and the
Details number is how the statement tells them apart.
Details
Details More will go here. The dialog is the place for anything true of one
Details account and of nothing else -- which CSV profile its downloads are in,
Details what its statements call a payment to it -- which is why it is a
Details dialog per account rather than another column in the account list.
Details
Details An OFX download names an account in the bank's own terms, a routing
Details number and an account number, and matching those to an account is the
Details mapping in ImportOfx. What is kept here is what a person reads off a
Details statement. The two are kept apart because a routing number is not a
Details bank's name and nobody should have to type one to label an account.

Attributes AccountSettings
Description What the dialog of one account holds.
| Attribute     | Type        | Default |
| Path          | AccountPath |         |
| Bank          | Text        | none    |
| AccountNumber | Text        | none    |

$WIP
Scenario An account starts with nothing known about it but its name
Description Nothing is required. An account with no bank and no number works
Description exactly as it does now, and the dialog is somewhere to put the
Description answer when there is one.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path            | Type | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking | Checking | false       |
When account settings opened : AccountSelect
| Path            |
| Assets:Checking |
Then account settings are : AccountSettings Vertical
| Path          | Assets:Checking |
| Bank          | none            |
| AccountNumber | none            |

$WIP
Scenario The bank and the number are kept with the book
Description Written when they are entered, because a book is a file and not a
Description session. See the saving rule in Books.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path            | Type | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking | Checking | false       |
When account settings changed : AccountSettings Vertical
| Path          | Assets:Checking |
| Bank          | First Bank      |
| AccountNumber | 1234567890      |
When the book is reopened
Then account settings are : AccountSettings Vertical
| Path          | Assets:Checking |
| Bank          | First Bank      |
| AccountNumber | 1234567890      |

$WIP
Scenario An account number is kept as it is written
Description A number on a statement is a label and not an amount: it may carry
Description dashes or spaces, it may have a leading zero that matters, and it
Description is never added to anything. So it is kept exactly as typed, with
Description only the blanks at either end taken off.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path             | Type       | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking  | Checking   | false       |
| Liabilities:Visa | CreditCard | false       |
When account settings changed : AccountSettings Vertical
| Path          | Assets:Checking |
| Bank          | First Bank      |
| AccountNumber | 0012-3456       |
When account settings changed : AccountSettings Vertical
| Path          | Liabilities:Visa    |
| Bank          | Second Bank         |
| AccountNumber | ~4111 1111 1111 1111 |
Then account settings are : AccountSettings
| Path             | Bank        | AccountNumber       |
| Assets:Checking  | First Bank  | 0012-3456           |
| Liabilities:Visa | Second Bank | 4111 1111 1111 1111 |
# The tilde in the table is a space, so the second number is written with a
# leading blank and is kept without it. The leading zeros of the first survive,
# which they would not if a number were read as a number.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Finding a category
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

$WIP
BusinessRule Typing part of a category offers it and everything under it
Description Categorising a transaction by hand starts with typing. Whatever is
Description typed is matched against every part of every path, and a category
Description that matches is offered along with all the categories beneath it --
Description because typing a parent is how a person says "somewhere in here".
Details Matching is on any segment, not only the first, so "fuel" finds
Details Expenses:Auto:Fuel without anyone having typed Expenses or Auto. It
Details ignores case, because nobody capitalises while typing fast.
Details
Details Offering the children of a match is the part that matters. There are
Details nearly four hundred categories here and the useful ones sit two levels
Details down; a picker that offered only exact matches would mean typing the
Details whole path, and one that offered only leaves would make a parent
Details untypeable.
Details
Details A hidden category is not offered. Nor is one that has not been used for
Details years: the book has categories last used in 2008, and a list that offers
Details them alongside the ones in weekly use is a list nobody reads to the
Details bottom. Both stay in the book and both keep their history -- this is
Details about what is offered while typing, not about what exists.
Examples: CategoryMatch
| Typed | Offered                                            | Notes                                  |
| fuel  | Expenses:Auto:Fuel                                 | Any segment, not just the first        |
| FUEL  | Expenses:Auto:Fuel                                 | Case is ignored                        |
| auto  | Expenses:Auto, and every category under it         | Typing a parent means somewhere in here |
| @FU   | Expenses:Auto:Fuel                                 | An alias stands for one category        |
| zzz   | Nothing                                            | No match is not an error               |

Attributes CategoryMatch
| Attribute | Type        | Default |
| Typed     | SearchText  |         |
| Offered   | String      |         |
| Notes     | String      | none    |

$WIP
Scenario Typing part of a name offers the category and its children
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                    | Type    | Placeholder | Hidden |
| Expenses:Auto           | Expense | true        | false  |
| Expenses:Auto:Fuel      | Expense | false       | false  |
| Expenses:Auto:Insurance | Expense | false       | false  |
| Expenses:Groceries      | Expense | false       | false  |
When category typed : CategorySearch Vertical
| Typed | auto |
Then categories offered are : CategoryOffered
| Path                    |
| Expenses:Auto           |
| Expenses:Auto:Fuel      |
| Expenses:Auto:Insurance |
# Groceries is not offered: nothing in its path matches.

Attributes CategorySearch
Description What has been typed so far, and whether the search was asked to
Description include the categories it normally leaves out.
| Attribute      | Type       | Default |
| Typed          | SearchText |         |
| IncludeUnused  | Boolean    | false   |

Attributes CategoryOffered
| Attribute | Type        | Default |
| Path      | AccountPath |         |

$WIP
Scenario An alias stands for one category however little is typed
Description A category used weekly should not need its name typed. The alias is
Description the user's own, a couple of letters after an at sign, and it offers
Description exactly the one category rather than everything that matches.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                    | Type    | Placeholder | Alias |
| Expenses:Auto           | Expense | true        | none  |
| Expenses:Auto:Fuel      | Expense | false       | @FU   |
| Expenses:Auto:Insurance | Expense | false       | none  |
When category typed : CategorySearch Vertical
| Typed | @FU |
Then categories offered are : CategoryOffered
| Path               |
| Expenses:Auto:Fuel |
# Only the one. An alias is a person saying exactly which category they mean,
# so the children are not brought along as they are for a typed parent.

$WIP
Scenario A category not used for years is left out while typing
Description It is still in the book, still has its history, and still appears in
Description a report that covers the years it was used. It is simply not put in
Description front of someone categorising a transaction today.
Given today is : Today Vertical
| Date | 2026-10-05 |
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type    | Placeholder |
| Expenses:Auto          | Expense | true        |
| Expenses:Auto:Fuel     | Expense | false       |
| Expenses:Auto:Carriage | Expense | false       |
Given dated postings are : DatedPostingLine
| Date       | Account                | Amount |
| 2026-09-01 | Expenses:Auto:Fuel     | 40.00  |
| 2009-04-02 | Expenses:Auto:Carriage | 12.00  |
When category typed : CategorySearch Vertical
| Typed | auto |
Then categories offered are : CategoryOffered
| Path               |
| Expenses:Auto      |
| Expenses:Auto:Fuel |
# Carriage last saw a posting in 2009, so it is not offered. Asking to see
# everything brings it back.

$WIP
Scenario Asking for the unused ones brings them back
Given today is : Today Vertical
| Date | 2026-10-05 |
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type    | Placeholder |
| Expenses:Auto          | Expense | true        |
| Expenses:Auto:Fuel     | Expense | false       |
| Expenses:Auto:Carriage | Expense | false       |
Given dated postings are : DatedPostingLine
| Date       | Account                | Amount |
| 2026-09-01 | Expenses:Auto:Fuel     | 40.00  |
| 2009-04-02 | Expenses:Auto:Carriage | 12.00  |
When category typed : CategorySearch Vertical
| Typed         | auto |
| IncludeUnused | true |
Then categories offered are : CategoryOffered
| Path                   |
| Expenses:Auto          |
| Expenses:Auto:Carriage |
| Expenses:Auto:Fuel     |
# Which is the point of it being a search and not a deletion.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What a balance is
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

DomainTerm RawBalance : Money
Description The sum of the amounts of every posting to one account, with the
Description sign those postings carry. Debits are positive throughout.

DomainTerm DisplayBalance : Money
Description The raw balance multiplied by the display sign of the account type,
Description so that each account reads the way its own statement reads.

BusinessRule Display sign follows from account type
Description Which account types read the other way round from their postings.
Details A charge to a credit card credits that account, leaving a negative raw
Details balance, but the cardholder thinks of it as an amount owed. Income is
Details credited by a paycheque, and a report should show it as earned.
Examples: DisplaySignOf
| AccountType        | DisplaySign | Notes                                       |
| Checking           | 1           | Positive raw balance means money held       |
| Savings            | 1           |                                             |
| Cash               | 1           |                                             |
| Brokerage          | 1           |                                             |
| IRA                | 1           |                                             |
| HSA                | 1           |                                             |
| Retirement401k     | 1           |                                             |
| RealEstate         | 1           |                                             |
| Vehicle            | 1           |                                             |
| OtherAsset         | 1           |                                             |
| AccountsReceivable | 1           | Owed to the business, so money held         |
| TransferIn         | 1           | Off the balance sheet, and still money held |
| Expense            | 1           | Positive raw balance means money spent      |
| CreditCard         | -1          | Shown as the amount owed                    |
| LineOfCredit       | -1          |                                             |
| Mortgage           | -1          |                                             |
| AutoLoan           | -1          |                                             |
| PersonalLoan       | -1          |                                             |
| OtherDebt          | -1          | Shown as the amount owed                    |
| AccountsPayable    | -1          | Owed by the business                        |
| TransferOut        | -1          |                                             |
| Income             | -1          | Shown as the amount earned                  |
| Equity             | -1          | Shown as the amount contributed to the book |
# The sign is the balance side, for everything but the three categories: what
# is owned reads positive and what is owed reads as the amount owed. So there
# is one rule behind two tables, and the balance-side rule in CoreTypes is it.

Attributes DisplaySignOf
| Attribute   | Type        | Default |
| AccountType | AccountType | Checking |
| DisplaySign | Integer     | 1       |
| Notes       | String      | none    |

Define SomePostings
Description The postings the next three scenarios all read, named so that each
Description can ask for them. A scenario cannot take a table from the scenario
Description before it: every generated test stands alone.
| Account            | Amount   |
| Assets:Checking    | 1000.00  |
| Assets:Checking    | -45.00   |
| Liabilities:Visa   | -120.00  |
| Liabilities:Visa   | 20.00    |
| Income:Salary      | -1000.00 |
| Expenses:Groceries | 45.00    |
| Expenses:Auto:Fuel | 100.00   |

Scenario Balances of an account after some postings
Given the chart of accounts is as previous
Given postings are : PostingLine
=SomePostings
Then balances are : AccountBalance
| Account            | RawBalance | DisplayBalance | Notes            |
| Assets:Checking    | 955.00     | 955.00         | Money held       |
| Liabilities:Visa   | -100.00    | 100.00         | Owed on the card |
| Income:Salary      | -1000.00   | 1000.00        | Earned           |
| Expenses:Groceries | 45.00      | 45.00          | Spent            |
| Expenses:Auto:Fuel | 100.00     | 100.00         | Spent            |

Attributes PostingLine
| Attribute | Type        | Default |
| Account   | AccountPath |         |
| Amount    | Money       | 0.00    |

Attributes AccountBalance
| Attribute      | Type        | Default |
| Account        | AccountPath |         |
| RawBalance     | Money       | 0.00    |
| DisplayBalance | Money       | 0.00    |
| Notes          | String      | none    |

Scenario A placeholder balance is the total of its descendants
Description A placeholder takes no postings of its own, so its balance is
Description entirely the roll-up. A node that is both posted to and a parent
Description reports both numbers, which is why there are two columns.
Given the chart of accounts is as previous
Given postings are : PostingLine
=SomePostings
Then rolled up balances are : RolledUpBalance
| Account            | OwnBalance | TotalBalance | Notes                       |
| Expenses:Auto:Fuel | 100.00     | 100.00       | A leaf                      |
| Expenses:Auto      | 0.00       | 100.00       | Placeholder: all from below |
| Expenses:Groceries | 45.00      | 45.00        |                             |
| Expenses           | 0.00       | 145.00       | Groceries and Auto          |
| Assets             | 0.00       | 955.00       |                             |

Attributes RolledUpBalance
| Attribute    | Type        | Default |
| Account      | AccountPath |         |
| OwnBalance   | Money       | 0.00    |
| TotalBalance | Money       | 0.00    |
| Notes        | String      | none    |

Scenario Net worth is the total of the real accounts
Description The nominal accounts are left out: including them would always give
Description zero, because every transaction balances.
Given the chart of accounts is as previous
Given postings are : PostingLine
=SomePostings
Then net worth is : NetWorth Vertical
| Assets      | 955.00 |
| Liabilities | 100.00 |
| NetWorth    | 855.00 |

Attributes NetWorth
| Attribute   | Type  | Default |
| Assets      | Money | 0.00    |
| Liabilities | Money | 0.00    |
| NetWorth    | Money | 0.00    |

Scenario A balance as at a date ignores later postings
Description Every balance in the program is a balance as at a date; the figure
Description shown without one is the balance as at today. See Reports.
Given the chart of accounts is as previous
Given dated postings are : DatedPostingLine
| Date       | Account         | Amount  |
| 2024-01-05 | Assets:Checking | 1000.00 |
| 2024-02-10 | Assets:Checking | -45.00  |
| 2024-03-20 | Assets:Checking | -55.00  |
Then balance as at dates are : BalanceAsAt
| AsAt       | Account         | Balance | Notes                       |
| 2024-01-04 | Assets:Checking | 0.00    | Before anything was posted  |
| 2024-01-05 | Assets:Checking | 1000.00 | The date itself is included |
| 2024-02-28 | Assets:Checking | 955.00  |                             |
| 2024-12-31 | Assets:Checking | 900.00  | Everything                  |

Attributes DatedPostingLine
| Attribute | Type        | Default    |
| Date      | Date        | 2024-01-01 |
| Account   | AccountPath |            |
| Amount    | Money       | 0.00       |

Attributes BalanceAsAt
| Attribute | Type        | Default    |
| AsAt      | Date        | 2024-01-01 |
| Account   | AccountPath |            |
| Balance   | Money       | 0.00       |
| Notes     | String      | none       |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Opening balances
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Scenario Opening a real account with a balance
Description There is no opening balance field on an account. An opening balance
Description is a transaction against Equity:Opening Balances, so the book
Description balances from its first day. This is what lets an import of twenty
Description years of history start from a known position.
Given the chart of accounts is as previous
When account added with an opening balance : AccountWithOpeningBalance Vertical
| Path           | Assets:Brokerage Cash |
| Type           | Checking              |
| OpeningBalance | 2500.00               |
| OpeningDate    | 2005-01-01            |
Then postings created are : DatedPostingLine
| Date       | Account                 | Amount   |
| 2005-01-01 | Assets:Brokerage Cash   | 2500.00  |
| 2005-01-01 | Equity:Opening Balances | -2500.00 |

Attributes AccountWithOpeningBalance
| Attribute      | Type        | Default    |
| Path           | AccountPath |            |
| Type           | AccountType | Checking   |
| OpeningBalance | Money       | 0.00       |
| OpeningDate    | Date        | 2005-01-01 |

Scenario Opening a credit card with a balance owed
Description The opening balance is entered the way the statement reads, as an
Description amount owed, and is stored with the sign the posting needs.
Given the chart of accounts is as previous
When account added with an opening balance : AccountWithOpeningBalance Vertical
| Path           | Liabilities:Amex |
| Type           | CreditCard       |
| OpeningBalance | 300.00           |
| OpeningDate    | 2005-01-01       |
Then postings created are : DatedPostingLine
| Date       | Account                 | Amount  |
| 2005-01-01 | Liabilities:Amex        | -300.00 |
| 2005-01-01 | Equity:Opening Balances | 300.00  |
Then balances are : AccountBalance
| Account          | RawBalance | DisplayBalance | Notes            |
| Liabilities:Amex | -300.00    | 300.00         | Owed, as entered |

Scenario An account opened with no balance posts nothing
Given the chart of accounts is as previous
When account added with an opening balance : AccountWithOpeningBalance Vertical
| Path           | Assets:Money Market |
| Type           | Checking            |
| OpeningBalance | 0.00                |
| OpeningDate    | 2005-01-01          |
Then postings created are : DatedPostingLine
| Date | Account | Amount |
# No rows: an opening balance of zero is not worth a transaction.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Changing the type of an account
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Scenario The type of an unused account may be changed
Given the chart of accounts is as previous
Given Expenses:Auto:Repairs has postings : PostingCount
| Count |
| 0     |
When account type changed : AccountTypeChange Vertical
| Path    | Expenses:Auto:Repairs |
| NewType | Income                |
Then account is : Account Vertical CompareOnly
| Path | Expenses:Auto:Repairs |
| Type | Income                |

Attributes AccountTypeChange
| Attribute | Type        | Default |
| Path      | AccountPath |         |
| NewType   | AccountType | Checking |

Scenario Changing type across the real and nominal divide is refused
Description Changing an Expense into a Bank would change the sign that every
Description existing posting is read with, and so rewrite history in silence.
Given the chart of accounts is as previous
Given Expenses:Groceries has postings : PostingCount
| Count |
| 412   |
When account type changed : AccountTypeChange Vertical
| Path    | Expenses:Groceries |
| NewType | Checking           |
Then rejected because : Rejection
| Reason                                                               |
| Expenses:Groceries has postings; a category cannot become an account |

Scenario Changing type within the real accounts is allowed
Description A Bank mistakenly set up as Cash is a common correction, and the
Description display sign is the same for both, so no posting changes meaning.
Given the chart of accounts is as previous
Given Assets:Savings has postings : PostingCount
| Count |
| 96    |
When account type changed : AccountTypeChange Vertical
| Path    | Assets:Savings |
| NewType | Cash           |
Then account is : Account Vertical CompareOnly
| Path | Assets:Savings |
| Type | Cash           |
